The massless field equation is
$$
\boxed{\phi''+2\mathcal H\phi'-\nabla^2\phi=0},
\qquad \mathcal H=\frac{a'}a.
$$
For a homogeneous solution this becomes
$$
(a^2\bar\phi')'=0.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\bar\phi'\propto a^{-2}},
\qquad \boxed{p=-2}.
$$
Since $\dot{\bar\phi}=\bar\phi'/a$, the cosmic-time velocity decays as $a^{-3}$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The second Friedmann equation gives the <first Hubble slow-roll parameter>
$$
\boxed{\epsilon=-\frac{\dot H}{H^2}
=\frac{\dot{\bar\phi}^{,2}}{2M_{\rm Pl}^2H^2}}.
$$
Because $\dot{\bar\phi}\propto a^{-3}$,
$$
\frac{\dot\epsilon}{\epsilon H}
=2\frac{\ddot{\bar\phi}}{H\dot{\bar\phi}}
-2\frac{\dot H}{H^2}
=-6+2\epsilon.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\eta_{\rm SR}=-6+2\epsilon}.
$$
As $a\to\infty$, the kinetic energy decays as $a^{-6}$ and the constant $\Lambda$ dominates. Hence $H$ approaches a constant,
$$
\boxed{aH\simeq-\frac1\eta,
\qquad\epsilon\simeq0,
\qquad\eta_{\rm SR}\simeq-6}.
$$
This non-attractor background is <ultra-slow-roll inflation>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

